WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.148
Parameterized queries are the primary prevention pattern for raw SQL.

2
00:00:04.148 --> 00:00:11.613
The core idea is simple: SQL structure is defined by the developer, and
data values are passed separately to the database driver.

3
00:00:11.613 --> 00:00:18.941
The database driver or prepared statement mechanism then treats those
values as data, not as syntax that changes the command.

4
00:00:18.941 --> 00:00:24.747
This separation is more reliable than trying to clean or manually rewrite
values into a safe string.

5
00:00:24.747 --> 00:00:31.176
Placeholders and parameter binding vary across database drivers and
libraries, but the principle is stable.

6
00:00:31.176 --> 00:00:35.600
The application supplies a query shape and a separate collection of
values.

7
00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:39.264
The driver binds those values through the database interface.

8
00:00:39.264 --> 00:00:47.006
Developers should follow the parameter style required by the chosen
library rather than inventing string formatting conventions.

9
00:00:47.006 --> 00:00:55.302
If a value came from a request, import, partner response, or stored field,
it should normally flow through binding rather than concatenation.

10
00:00:55.302 --> 00:01:00.002
Parameterized queries apply to SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE
operations.

11
00:01:00.002 --> 00:01:02.906
They are not only for login checks or search boxes.

12
00:01:02.906 --> 00:01:09.611
Any operation that includes external or variable values should use the
safe parameter mechanism for those values.

13
00:01:09.611 --> 00:01:17.630
This includes identifiers used as lookup values, timestamps, status
values, tenant IDs, account IDs, text fields, and numeric filters.

14
00:01:17.630 --> 00:01:22.952
The safest standard is that raw SQL without parameters should be unusual
and easy to review.

15
00:01:22.952 --> 00:01:28.828
Escaping is not the preferred primary defense because it is context-
dependent and easy to get wrong.

16
00:01:28.828 --> 00:01:34.013
Different databases, encodings, settings, and query contexts can behave
differently.

17
00:01:34.013 --> 00:01:39.128
Parameterization delegates value handling to the database driver in the
intended way.

18
00:01:39.128 --> 00:01:51.640
Developers still need to review SQL structure, dynamic identifiers, and
authorization, but parameter binding removes a large class of mistakes
where data values are accidentally interpreted as part of a command.
